The Stranger's storm, redrawn: mockup round 3 (waiting for .go before wiring)

Branch stills/stranger-storm-art, folder stranger-storm-art/round3/ (rounds 1 and 2 stay beside it). Two versions, Eric picks:

A, painterly
  A-painterly-phone-fire-turn.gif / .mp4       phone, light, the storm's 1st turn (fire leads), the rift whole
  A-painterly-computer-ice-turn.gif / .mp4     computer, light, the storm's 1st turn (ice leads), the rift whole
  A-painterly-phone-three-turns.png            the storm's three turns side by side: the rift whole, closing, nearly healed
  A-painterly-computer-fire-turn.png           computer, light, the 2nd turn (fire), full screen
B, the GBA pass
  B-gba-phone-fire-turn.gif / .mp4, B-gba-computer-ice-turn.gif / .mp4, B-gba-phone-three-turns.png, B-gba-computer-fire-turn.png
The MP4s and PNGs show the true pixels and colors; the GIFs are 256 colors and scaled down.

What changed since round 2 (Eric, 18:35: the crack "looks like a joke ... squiggly lines ... like somebody ripped open the board")
- The crack is now drawn art, rendered offline like the ring (scripts/storm-art/rift.py): a deep jagged rift down the board's middle,
  between his two swords, about a square across at its widest. Looking down into it: terraced walls stepping down into the dark,
  basalt with veins of lava on the fire side, blue ice on the other, a white-hot seam in the deep where the two meet. Its lips are
  lifted, broken slabs (lit at the edge, shaded toward a break line, a thin shadow past it), thin cracks run off into the board,
  molten rock and scorch spill over the fire side's lip with an orange glow on the squares, ice crystals and rime grow from the
  other with a cold glow, and chips of board (in the board's two colors, with thickness and a shadow) float over it.
- Three heal stages, one each turn of the storm: 88% of the board's height and whole; 64% and two-thirds as wide with fewer chips and
  crystals; 40% and under half as wide; then gone with the storm.
- It sits under the pieces. The last-move highlight and a fire turn's marked tiles stay over it (they must stay readable).
- Both styles: A at 640 px, B at the ring's own pixel size (15 colors, dithered).
- The ring, the dim (22%) and the see-through (88%) are as in round 2.

What would ship
- A: storm.webp 1029 KB + frost.webp 34 KB + three rift stills about 92 KB (about 26 MB decoded for the loop).
- B: storm.webp 376 KB + frost.webp 1 KB + three rift stills about 6 KB (about 12 MB decoded). B is the lighter one.
- At play time: 2 to 6 image draws a frame for the ring, no particles; the rift is one still image a turn; the dim one CSS layer.

Calls for the director / Eric
1. A or B.
2. The rift runs top to bottom between the swords (the fire world left of it, the ice world right). If his reference runs the other
   way (side to side), it's one setting in rift.py: say so.
3. In the opening scene the rift would open as the swords go in (in place of today's line cracks) and stay; the ripples stay.
